DOMAIN: Dialectics of Nature and Engels' Metaphysics/Epistemology
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-11
PAPERS_FOUND: 13 total (High: 7, Medium: 5, Low: 1)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Engels' Dialectics of Nature (posthumously published, composed 1873-1882) and
Anti-Duhring (1878) constitute the foundational texts for a systematic materialist
philosophy of nature grounded in three "laws" of dialectics: the transformation of
quantity into quality, the interpenetration of opposites, and the negation of the
negation. Engels aimed to show that these laws govern the natural world independently
of human consciousness, engaging extensively with 19th-century biology (Darwin),
thermodynamics, and chemistry.

The domain is defined by a fundamental split. Western Marxists -- above all Lukacs
in History and Class Consciousness (1923) -- argued that dialectics is an epistemological
method applicable only to the subject-object relation in history and society; extending
it to nature commits a category error by treating nature as a subject of historical
development. This became the majority position in Western Marxism (Korsch, Gramsci,
Frankfurt School). The opposing tradition, drawing on Engels, Lenin, and Soviet
diamat, maintains that dialectics describes objective laws of material reality as such.
Rehabilitations of Engels' approach have come from critical realists (Collier, Benton),
eco-Marxists (Foster), and recent textual scholars (Kangal).

Recent scholarship has shifted from the old polarized debate toward more nuanced
historical and philosophical reconstructions: Kangal's 2020 monograph offers a
hermeneutically careful reading that acknowledges incompleteness in Engels' project
while rehabilitating its philosophical ambitions. Shafer (2023) recovers Engels'
importance for critical theory by reading him alongside Horkheimer. The eco-Marxist
current (Foster 2022) argues that dialectics of nature is essential for addressing
contemporary ecological crises. Blackledge (2025) reviews and criticizes the
dismissive readings of Engels represented by Liedman.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is central to the overall Engels project: it concerns whether Engels'
philosophy of nature is a coherent extension of Marx's materialism or an alien
importation that distorts historical materialism by making it dependent on a
speculative naturalism. The question of whether history's dialectic requires nature's
dialectic bears directly on Engels' philosophy of history and its defensibility.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The reception of Timpanaro's biological materialism in the Anglophone debate remains
underexplored. Systematic treatment of the relationship between Engels' philosophy of
science and positivism (Comte, Spencer) is also relatively rare. The Arencibia Ilyenkov
paper has no abstract available.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The domain maps cleanly onto a debate between (a) restricting dialectics to
history/society (Lukacs, Western Marxism), (b) extending it to nature (Soviet diamat,
eco-Marxists, critical realists), and (c) reconstructing what Engels actually argued
without assimilating it to either camp (Kangal, Shafer). The synthesis should trace
how these positions determine opposed interpretations of Engels' philosophy of history.
====================================================================
}

@book{kangal2020friedrich,
  author = {Kangal, Kaan},
  title = {Friedrich Engels and the Dialectics of Nature},
  year = {2020},
  publisher = {Springer International Publishing},
  series = {Marx, Engels, and Marxisms},
  doi = {10.1007/978-3-030-34335-4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Engels' Dialectics of Nature is neither the systematic completion of a dialectical materialist ontology that Soviet readers claimed nor the philosophically bankrupt scientism that Western Marxist critics dismissed; instead it is an unfinished project whose intentions can be reconstructed through a historical hermeneutic that distinguishes authorial, textual, editorial, and interpretational layers.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most thorough scholarly reconstruction of what Engels was actually attempting in Dialectics of Nature, documenting the composition history and identifying which philosophical problems Engels solved and which he left open. Essential for any assessment of whether the three laws of dialectics constitute a defensible general ontology.

  POSITION: Defends a nuanced rehabilitation of Engels against both Soviet dogmatism and Western-Marxist dismissal.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, Engels, three-laws-dialectics, historiography, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{kangal2019intentions,
  author = {Kangal, Kaan},
  title = {Engels' Intentions in Dialectics of Nature},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {83},
  number = {2},
  pages = {215--243},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.2019.83.2.215},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the debate over Engels' Dialectics of Nature by distinguishing between what Engels actually intended and what interpreters have attributed to him; argues that Engels' dialectics is less complete than commonly supposed but more philosophically substantive than most critics allow.

  RELEVANCE: The journal article companion to the 2020 monograph; more focused on the hermeneutic methodology and the historical reception of the text. Directly addresses the Marx-Engels split debate that structures the broader domain.

  POSITION: Historical hermeneutic approach, rehabilitative but not hagiographic.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, Engels, hermeneutics, reception-history, High},
  abstract = {Reading different or controversial intentions into Marx and Engels’ works has been somewhat a common but rather unquestioned practice in the history of Marxist scholarship. Engels’ Dialectics of Nature, a torso for some and a great book for others, is a case in point. A bold line seems to shape the entire Engels debate and separate two opposite views in this regard: Engels the contaminator of Marx's materialism vs. Engels the self-started genius of dialectical materialism. What Engels, unlike Marx, has not enjoyed so far is a critical reflection upon the relationship between different layers of this text: authorial, textual, editorial and interpretational. Informed by a historical hermeneutic, inquiry into the elements that structure the debate on “Dialectics of Nature,” and into the different political and philosophical functions attached to it, makes it possible to relocate the meaning of “dialectics” in a more precise context. Engels’ dialectics is less complete than we usually think it is, but he achieved more than most scholars would like to admit.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{foster2022return,
  author = {Foster, John Bellamy},
  title = {The Return of the Dialectics of Nature},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2022},
  pages = {1--26},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-20222279},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the ecological crisis of the Anthropocene makes the dialectics of nature concept newly urgent; traces how the concept was reduced to Stalinist dogma in official Marxism and rejected wholesale in Western Marxism, and proposes its rehabilitation as the basis for a historical-materialist ecological synthesis.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the eco-Marxist defense of Engels' dialectics of nature, directly confronting the Western Marxist rejection (Lukacs' position). Argues that the divorce between history and nature in Western Marxism is itself an ideological position that cripples ecological thought.

  POSITION: Pro-Engels ecological rehabilitation, against both Soviet dogmatism and Western Marxist rejection.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, ecology, Western-Marxism, Lukacs, High},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 The resurrection of the classical Marxian ecological critique in the context of the current planetary emergency has led to the return of the concept of the dialectics of nature, associated with the work of Frederick Engels in particular. In the century following the deaths of Charles Darwin and Karl Marx, the dialectics-of-nature conception played a formative role in the development of the modern ecological critique within science, notably in Britain, and helped inspire the contemporary environmentalist movement. Nevertheless, all of this occurred outside the dominant streams of Marxian thought and practice, where a great chasm had arisen in this area. Whereas official Marxism in the Soviet Union reduced the dialectics of nature to a fixed dogma, Western Marxism rejected it altogether. In the current Anthropocene Epoch in the geological time scale – and in what is referred to here as the Capitalinian Age of the Anthropocene – a new historical-materialist synthesis constructed on classical foundations, reintegrating the dialectics of nature, so as to address the immense ecological challenges confronting humanity, is seen as objectively (and subjectively) necessary.},
}

@article{blackledge2017practical,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Practical Materialism: Engels's \textit{Anti-D\"{u}hring} as Marxist Philosophy},
  journal = {Critique},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {45},
  number = {4},
  pages = {483--499},
  doi = {10.1080/03017605.2017.1377927},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Defends Anti-Duhring as a philosophically coherent and politically significant text against the prevailing view that Engels distorted Marx's materialism by constructing a systematic dialectical materialism; argues that Engels' practical materialism captures something essential to Marxism that critics have missed.

  RELEVANCE: Anti-Duhring is the published systematic statement of Engels' three laws and his account of the relation between dialectics of nature and historical materialism; this article provides a careful philosophical defense of that text, directly relevant to assessing Engels' epistemology.

  POSITION: Rehabilitative reading of Engels' systematic philosophy against Althusserian and Western Marxist dismissals.
  },
  keywords = {Anti-Duhring, dialectical-materialism, Engels, three-laws, High},
  abstract = {Frederick Engels’s Anti-Dühring was the most important theoretical response to the emerging reformist tendencies within European socialism in the 19th century. It also proved to be Engels’s most influential, and controversial work. Because it is, as Hal Draper points out, ‘the only more or less systematic presentation of Marxism’ by either by Marx or Engels, anyone wanting to reinterpret Marx must first detach it from his seal of approval. It is thus around Anti-Dühring and related texts that debates about the relationship of Marx to ‘Engelsian’ Marxism have tended to focus. This essay re-engages with debates about Engels’s mature work with a view to unpicking his contribution to Marxism from caricatured criticisms of his thought.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{shafer2023engels,
  author = {Shafer, Matt},
  title = {Engels after Frankfurt: Nature and Enlightenment in Critical Theory},
  journal = {New German Critique},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {50},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--29},
  doi = {10.1215/0094033x-10140705},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs the Frankfurt Institute's early publication of Engels' Dialectics of Nature manuscripts in the 1920s and traces how the Frankfurt School subsequently turned away from Engels' philosophy of natural science; argues that a recovery of Engels' naturalism can help critical theory move beyond the pessimism about technoscience that characterized Horkheimer's mature work.

  RELEVANCE: Bridges the gap between Engels' dialectics of nature and critical theory, showing that the Western Marxist rejection was historically specific rather than philosophically inevitable. Directly addresses whether Engels' naturalism is compatible with a sophisticated critical epistemology.

  POSITION: Qualified rehabilitation of Engels' philosophy of nature within the critical theory tradition.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, critical-theory, Frankfurt-School, Horkheimer, naturalism, High},
  abstract = {The Institute for Social Research in Frankfurt facilitated the first publication of Friedrich Engels’s controversial Dialectics of Nature manuscripts in the 1920s. Yet the subsequent work of the institute’s most influential members almost entirely turned away from the approach to natural science that Engels had advocated. The result was an indispensably incisive critique of social domination and a deepening skepticism about natural-scientific contributions to the construction of the postcapitalist alternative. Through a new reading of the development of Max Horkheimer’s analysis of empiricism and an original reconstruction of Engels’s unjustly maligned philosophy of nature, this essay outlines how critical theory can move beyond the pessimism about technoscientific practice that characterized the Frankfurt School’s most influential early work—without forfeiting the historical insight such pessimism once animated.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{blackledge2025dialectics,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Engels, the Dialectics of Nature, and the Birth of ``Marxism''},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {89},
  number = {1},
  pages = {85--98},
  doi = {10.1177/00368237241312552},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reviews and critiques Sven-Eric Liedman's The Game of Contradictions (2022), a dismissive reading of Engels as a system-builder who damaged Marxism; argues that Liedman's focus on the negative aspects of system-building obscures the positive implications of Engels' historical naturalism for contemporary ecological thought.

  RELEVANCE: Engages directly with the most recent critical synthesis on Engels' dialectics of nature; provides a defense of Engels' approach to natural science as relevant rather than regressive.

  POSITION: Pro-Engels, eco-Marxist tradition.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, historical-naturalism, ecology, Engels-reception, High},
  abstract = {Sven-Eric Liedman’s The Game of Contradictions (2022) sets out to be both the most comprehensive study of Engels’s work on the social implications of 19th-century developments in the natural sciences and the most detailed analysis of this aspect of the Marx–Engels relationship. While noting the richness and depth of Liedman’s research, this essay argues that his critique of Engels as a system builder is one-sided. Liedman’s focus on the negative aspects of system building obscures the positive implications of Engels’s research. Far from damning 20th-century Marxism, Engels’s historical naturalism confirms that his ideas are more relevant than ever. The contemporary environmental crisis is simultaneously a natural and a capitalist crisis, and any adequate attempt to conceptualize it would be well advised to begin from insights both of his and Marx’s works. Unfortunately, despite highlighting overlaps between Marx’s and Engels’s approaches to the relation between social and natural sciences, Liedman’s dismissive approach to Engels’s Dialectics of Nature blinds him to the continuing relevance of this fundamental resource.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{azeri2022dialectics,
  author = {Azeri, Siyave\c{s}},
  title = {Engels' Dialectics of---Human Activity in---Nature},
  journal = {Marxism \& Sciences},
  year = {2022},
  pages = {69},
  doi = {10.56063/ms.2201.01105},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Challenges Western Marxist critiques (especially Lukacs) of Engels for allegedly non-dialectical treatment of praxis; argues that Engels' account of labour as a philosophical category anticipates Ilyenkov's concept of human activity and that the laws of dialectics are best understood as laws of human activity in social nature, not abstract laws of a nature external to society.

  RELEVANCE: Directly engages the Lukacs-versus-Engels debate over whether dialectics can apply to nature without collapsing the subject-object relation. Offers a reading that bridges the two camps by grounding nature-dialectics in praxis.

  POSITION: Defends Engels against Lukacs by reframing the dialectics of nature as praxis-centred.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, Lukacs, praxis, Ilyenkov, High},
  abstract = {: Several Marxist theorists criticize Engels for his supposed non-dialectical and non-philosophical conceptualization of “praxis,” which, according to Lukacs, amounts to Engels’ alleged misunderstanding of Kantian “thing-in-itself.” I argue that such “criticisms” of Engels is based on a dualistic understanding of practical vs. theoretical. Contrary to the critiques’ allegations, Engels provides an account of prax-is/labour as a philosophical category that is truthful to Marx’s and his own earlier efforts for constituting a materialist dialectics—one that anticipates the concept of “human activity” elaborated particularly in philosophical works of Evald Ilyenkov and psychological studies of Lev Vygotsky. According to Engels, the so-called laws of dialectics are historical thus depending on human activity as it is “with man [that] we enter history.” Furthermore, it is through labour (the highest form of human activity) that not only the human hands but also social humanity and human consciousness have been constituted. Thus, laws of dialectics are the most general laws of human activity in social nature. Engels, truthful to Marx’s materialist method, prioritizes action over substance.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{arencibia2021ilyenkov,
  author = {Piedra Arencibia, Rogney},
  title = {Ilyenkov's Dialectics of the Ideal and Engels's Dialectics of Nature},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {30},
  number = {3},
  pages = {145--177},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-12342048},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the influence of Engels on Ilyenkov's philosophy is systematically overlooked or denied by scholars who wish to assimilate Ilyenkov to Western Marxism; demonstrates that Ilyenkov, like Engels, conceives philosophy as Logic allied with natural science and develops an original cosmological hypothesis based on Engels' hierarchy of forms of matter's movement.

  RELEVANCE: Shows how Engels' dialectics of nature was productively developed in Soviet philosophy through Ilyenkov, providing an alternative lineage to the Western Marxist critique. Relevant to assessing whether there is a philosophically credible tradition of dialectical materialism in the natural sciences.

  POSITION: Rehabilitates the Soviet philosophical tradition by emphasising Engels-Ilyenkov continuity against Western Marxist appropriations.
  },
  keywords = {Ilyenkov, dialectics-of-nature, Soviet-Marxism, forms-of-matter, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract Within the current resurgence of interest in E.V. Ilyenkov, the influence of Engels on Ilyenkov’s work is either overlooked or denied, making Ilyenkov seem closer to Western Marxism than he actually is. In this paper, by considering Engels’s place in his philosophy, I show that Ilyenkov’s approach is fundamentally hostile to many of Western Marxism’s main views. Ilyenkov, like Engels, conceives philosophy as Logic and affirms the ‘alliance’ between philosophy and the natural sciences against speculative metaphysics. In this regard, he develops an original cosmological hypothesis based on Engels’s insights on the hierarchy of matter’s forms of movement and provides a remarkable account of the ideal as an attribute of nature that reproduces its concrete universality through social labour, the active transformation of nature’s phenomena into objects stamped with the seal of our subjectivity.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{zwart2020engels,
  author = {Zwart, Hub},
  title = {Friedrich Engels and the Technoscientific Reproducibility of Life: Synthetic Cells as Case Material for Practicing Dialectics of Science Today},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {84},
  number = {3},
  pages = {369--400},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.2020.84.3.369},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Engels' engagement with 19th-century natural science (cell physiology, thermodynamics, evolution) and proposes that a dialectical materialist understanding of contemporary technoscience can be built on this foundation; uses synthetic cell biology as a case study for how Engelsian dialectics operates in current scientific practice.

  RELEVANCE: Tests Engels' philosophy of natural science against contemporary scientific developments, addressing directly whether dialectics of nature is scientifically credible or merely a 19th-century curiosity.

  POSITION: Constructive application of Engels' philosophy of science to contemporary technoscience.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, philosophy-of-science, biology, thermodynamics, Medium},
  abstract = {Friedrich Engels’ dialectical assessment of modern science resulted from his fascination with the natural sciences (cell physiology, thermodynamics, theory of evolution) in combination with his resurging interest in the work of “old Hegel.” Engels became especially interested in what he saw as the molecular essence of life, namely proteins or, more specifically, albumin (Eiweiß), seeing life as the mode of existence of these enigmatic substances. Hegelian dialectics is crucial for a dialectical materialist understanding of contemporary technoscience. The dialectical materialist understanding of technoscience as a research practice builds on Engels, but also on later (scientific) authors who were inspired by his writings, e.g., life scientists such as Haldane and Bernal. Considering the criticism raised against Engels’ dialectics by 20th-century Marxists, a dialectical diagnostic of contemporary technoscience can be achieved, which shifts the focus from artificial albumin as “living matter” (as discussed by Engels) to contemporary research on synthetic cells (as anticipated by Engels).},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{rodriguez2023kuhnian,
  author = {Rodr{\'i}guez Chiarini, Sebasti{\'a}n Mat{\'i}as},
  title = {Engels' Dialectics of Nature as an Attempt at Scientific Normalization: A Kuhnian Reading},
  journal = {Signos Filos{\'o}ficos},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {25},
  number = {50},
  pages = {42--61},
  doi = {10.24275/sfilo.v25n50.04},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reads Engels' efforts in Anti-Duhring and Dialectics of Nature through Kuhn's concept of a disciplinary matrix, arguing that Engels was attempting to crystallise a new scientific paradigm for Marxism, not merely applying existing dialectics to natural data.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a philosophy-of-science reading that reframes the question of whether Engels' naturalism is defensible: instead of asking whether the three laws are metaphysically correct, asks whether they constitute a productive scientific research programme.

  POSITION: Kuhnian interpretation, neither simply defending nor rejecting Engels' naturalism.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, philosophy-of-science, Kuhn, paradigm, Medium},
  abstract = {In this paper I study Engels’ ideas on dialectical materialism, from the per-spective of Thomas Kuhn’s philosophy of science. I argue that Engels’ efforts, in works such as Anti-Düring or (even more so) in his Dialectics of Nature, are best understood as an attempt to crystalize a new scientific paradigm, in the sense that Kuhn (2000) gives to this last word. In order to do this, I start by identifying Marx’s previous statement regarding this topic. Then I present Kuhn’s concept of a “disciplinary matrix”. Thirdly, I apply the aforementioned concept to Engels’ work, identifying all the elements of the disciplinary matrix.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{barot2010sciences,
  author = {Barot, Emmanuel},
  title = {Sciences et dialectiques de la nature; La nature dans la pens\'ee dialectique},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {18},
  number = {2},
  pages = {143--164},
  doi = {10.1163/156920610x512499},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reviews Lucien Seve's edited collection on sciences and dialectics of nature and Eftichios Bitsakis' book on nature in dialectical thought; argues that Engels' dialectics of nature deserves rehabilitation from both Stalinist dogmatisation and Western Marxist dismissal, and that contemporary science offers resources for this.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a scholarly overview of the French-language debate on dialectics of nature, recovering positions that are often invisible in the Anglophone literature. Documents how the Stalinist dogmatisation of diamat made the entire project suspect even for sympathetic readers.

  POSITION: Rehabilitative, drawing on French Marxist philosophy of science tradition.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, French-Marxism, philosophy-of-science, reception, Medium},
  abstract = {Dialectics, especially Engels’s dialectics of nature, is nowadays mostly held in low esteem, even by Marxist scholars because of its Stalinist dogmatisation over the past century. The aim of this comparative review is to show some stakes and prospects, in Marxism and for Marxism, of the debate: the two reviewed books show how the dialectics of nature could, and why it should be considered in a renewed materialist approach to the natural sciences, and provides the reader with complementary outline from the cognitive sciences to physics, via mathematics.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{schmidt1962concept,
  author = {Schmidt, Alfred},
  title = {The Concept of Nature in Marx},
  year = {1962},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Marx's concept of nature is a sui generis materialist naturalism -- neither idealist nor mechanically materialist -- grounded in the concept of social metabolism (Stoffwechsel) between humanity and nature through labour; explicitly distinguishes Marx's approach from Engels' naturalistic extension and from Naturphilosophie.

  RELEVANCE: Schmidt's Frankfurt School-influenced reading established the interpretive baseline from which Lukacs-inspired critics argued that Engels distorted Marx by extending dialectics to nature. Essential for understanding what is at stake in the Marx-Engels debate on nature-dialectics.

  POSITION: Western Marxist tradition; draws a sharp line between Marx's epistemologically sophisticated naturalism and Engels' reductive ontological extension.
  },
  keywords = {nature, Marx, Engels, metabolism, Western-Marxism, Frankfurt-School, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{kuznetsov1971dialectics,
  author = {Kuznetsov, B. G.},
  title = {The Dialectics of Nature and Dialectics in Capital},
  journal = {Soviet Studies in Philosophy},
  year = {1971},
  volume = {10},
  number = {1},
  pages = {43--62},
  doi = {10.2753/rsp1061-1967100143},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues for a deep connection between Engels' philosophical generalisation of natural science in Dialectics of Nature and the philosophical categories of Marx's Capital; proposes that the dialectics of nature is not an external appendage to historical materialism but its necessary ontological foundation.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the Soviet philosophical tradition's case for the internal unity of dialectical materialism, directly opposed to Western Marxist readings that treat Engels' naturalism as a category error. Relevant to the question of whether history's dialectic requires nature's dialectic.

  POSITION: Soviet dialectical materialist tradition; defends the continuity of dialectics across nature and history.
  },
  keywords = {dialectics-of-nature, Capital, Soviet-Marxism, dialectical-materialism, Low},
  abstract = {A vast literature has been devoted to the Dialectics of Nature and dialectics in Capital. There is a considerable number of works in which the connection between the philosophical generalization of natural science in the Dialectics of Nature and the philosophical aspects of the economic categories in Capital are analyzed. I should like to touch upon only one aspect of the problem — that aspect which pertains to certain new problems in philosophical and economic thought. Reference is, first, to the new, unprecedented role of philosophy and natural science in the period of the scientific and technological revolution under the conditions of the unification and planning of science and, second, to the unprecedented dynamism of the economy today. This dynamism requires a radical rethinking of categories, and that rethinking, in turn, requires a return to the fundamental categories of Capital.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}
